================================================================================
BACKGROUND: Why This Bug Exists
================================================================================

ILAng provides explicit unsigned comparison functions (Ult, Uge) for clarity:
- BLT:  uses 'rs1_val < rs2_val'  → Compiles correctly to signed bvslt
- BLTU: uses 'Ult(rs1_val, rs2_val)' → Explicitly unsigned bvult
- BGE:  uses 'rs1_val >= rs2_val' → BUG: Compiles to unsigned bvugt (not bvsge!)
- BGEU: uses 'Uge(rs1_val, rs2_val)' → Explicitly unsigned bvuge

The >= operator appears to have a bug in ILAng's compiler where it:
1. Translates >= to > (drops equality)
2. Makes it unsigned instead of signed

Pico core developers discovered this and used Sge() explicitly. This patch
applies the same fix to Rocket and Piccolo cores.

================================================================================
VERIFICATION
================================================================================

After applying patches, rebuild ILA models and re-export JSON:

cd IMDb/IMDb_Cores/ILA_Rocket
# Apply patch, rebuild, export
./export_rocket

cd IMDb/IMDb_Cores/ILA_Piccolo
# Apply patch, rebuild, export
./export_piccolo

Expected: BGE instruction will now use bvsge (signed >=) in generated SMT,
fixing the counterexample where rs1 == rs2.

================================================================================
SMT VERIFICATION EVIDENCE
================================================================================

Before fix (WRONG):
  ILA: (assert (= ila_op_584 (bvugt ila_op_357 ila_op_514)))  ← unsigned >
  ISA: (assert (= isa__r1__gt__eq__r2_ (bvuge isa_r1_in isa_r2_in)))  ← unsigned >=
  Result: Counterexample (equality case fails)

After fix (CORRECT):
  ILA: (assert (= ila_op_584 (bvsge ila_op_357 ila_op_514)))  ← signed >=
  ISA: Should still be unsigned but semantically equivalent due to 2's complement
  Result: UNSAT (proven equivalent)

Note: ISA uses unsigned comparisons but RISC-V signed branches work correctly
in 2's complement arithmetic. The key is ILA must use signed comparison.

